<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      THE PARENT / AGGREGATOR POM  -  this is the first real difference
      from the monolith.

      The monolith has ONE pom producing ONE jar.
      Here the root pom produces NO jar at all: <packaging>pom</packaging>.
      Its job is to (a) list the modules and (b) hold shared configuration
      so every service agrees on Java version and Spring Boot version.

      `mvn test` at this level builds every module below in one go.
      ===================================================================
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>4.1.1</version>
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <groupId>com.persona</groupId>
    <artifactId>persona-microservices</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>
    <name>persona-microservices</name>
    <description>persona - the same app, split into independently deployable services</description>

    <properties>
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    </properties>

    <!--
      Day-00 had exactly one service. That was deliberate: a "microservice
      architecture" with one service is still a valid architecture, and it
      kept the slice small.

      THE DAY A SECOND SERVICE ARRIVES is when the architecture starts
      charging rent. With one service, "microservices" is a folder layout.
      With two that own separate databases, it is a set of things that have
      become impossible: no foreign key across the boundary, no join across
      it, and no single transaction covering an operation that spans both.

      `mvn test` at this level builds EVERY module. Note what that convenience
      hides: in production these are separate deployments with separate release
      cycles, and nothing guarantees they are ever the same version at the same
      time. The aggregator is a build-time fiction, useful and worth
      distrusting.

        <module>api-gateway</module>                (Day-17)
    -->
    <modules>
        <module>services/user-service</module>
    </modules>

</project>
